    <!-- FAQ Schema -->
    <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What should I do if someone is being electrocuted?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "First, ensure your own safety - never touch the person directly while they are in contact with the electrical source. Turn off the power at the circuit breaker or unplug the device. If you cannot disconnect the power, use a non-conducting object like a dry wooden board, rubber mat, or thick dry clothing to push the person away from the source. Once safe, call emergency services immediately and begin CPR if the person is not breathing or has no pulse."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How serious is an electric shock from household electricity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most minor household electric shocks (from standard 110-240V outlets) cause brief discomfort but no lasting harm, especially if contact is less than one second. However, any electrical shock can potentially be dangerous. Seek immediate medical attention if you experience chest pain, difficulty breathing, burns, irregular heartbeat, loss of consciousness, or if the shock lasted more than one second. Even seemingly minor shocks warrant medical evaluation if you have a pacemaker or other implanted device."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the long-term effects of electrical injury?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Long-term effects can appear days, months, or even years after the injury. These include chronic pain at the entry site, peripheral neuropathy (numbness, tingling, burning sensations), cardiovascular problems, cognitive difficulties (memory problems, difficulty concentrating), psychological effects (anxiety, depression, PTSD), and circulatory problems like Raynaud's phenomenon. Some survivors experience delayed neurological symptoms that develop weeks to months after the initial injury."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is electrical injury more dangerous in wet conditions?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Water dramatically reduces the electrical resistance of skin, allowing much more current to flow through the body. Dry skin has a resistance of about 100,000 ohms, while wet skin may have only 1,000 ohms - a 100-fold reduction. This means a shock in wet conditions delivers far more current to internal organs. Additionally, water conducts electricity, so standing in water while touching an electrical source creates a pathway for current to flow through your entire body."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When should I go to the hospital after an electric shock?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Seek immediate emergency care if you experience: loss of consciousness (even briefly), difficulty breathing or chest pain, burns at entry or exit points, muscle spasms or seizures, irregular heartbeat, confusion or memory problems, or if you have a pacemaker or ICD. Also seek care for any high-voltage exposure (greater than 500V), prolonged contact (more than 1 second), or if the current passed through your chest. Even without immediate symptoms, medical evaluation is recommended as some complications appear hours or days later."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the differences between low-voltage and high-voltage electrical injuries?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Low-voltage injuries (under 1000V, including household current) typically cause local burns and may trigger cardiac arrhythmias. High-voltage injuries (over 1000V) cause extensive deep tissue damage that may not be visible externally. The current travels through the body, destroying muscles, nerves, and blood vessels along its path. High-voltage injuries often require surgery, may lead to limb amputation, and carry a higher risk of delayed complications like cataracts and neurological damage."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    </script>

